  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Petticoat Mottlegill (Panaeolus papilionaceus) is a small, delicate mushroom characterized by its distinctive mottled gill pattern and bell-shaped cap. This species is commonly found throughout New England during wet periods and is notable for its ecological role in decomposing organic matter.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Mottled Gills",
      "description": "Gills display a distinctive two-toned or mottled appearance with darker and lighter patches, giving the mushroom its common name",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Bell-Shaped Cap",
      "description": "Cap is typically campanulate (bell-shaped) with a pale grayish-brown to tan color, usually 1-2 cm in diameter",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Thin Stem",
      "description": "Slender, fragile stem that is often darker at the base and lighter toward the top, typically 4-8 cm tall",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Petticoat Mottlegill thrives in moist environments such as manured fields, lawns, compost heaps, and decaying wood throughout New England, particularly during spring and fall.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "The name 'Petticoat Mottlegill' derives from the frilly appearance of its mottled gills, which resembles the ruffled edge of a petticoat garment.",
